{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 24, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a prominent figure in the 12th century Levant a ruler who navigated complex political landscapes and fostered a period of relative stability within his domain. He reigned as Count of Tripoli from 1183 until his death in 1203. His reign is notable for its military successes diplomatic endeavors and contributions to the cultural and economic life of the region.\n\n*Early Life and Ascendancy* Raymond was born in 1145 to Raymond II Count of Tripoli and his wife Eva of Blois. He inherited the countship upon his father's death in 1183. Immediately he faced challenges from neighboring powers particularly the Seljuk Sultanate of Rum and the Kingdom of Jerusalem. Raymond skillfully consolidated his power through a combination of military campaigns strategic alliances and astute diplomacy. He successfully defended Tripoli from Seljuk incursions and strengthened the city's defenses. He also actively engaged in conflicts with the Kingdom of Jerusalem often aligning himself with the latter to protect Tripoli’s interests. His early reign saw a period of significant growth and prosperity for Tripoli.\n\nRaymond III's reign was marked by military campaigns and diplomatic maneuvering. He fought against the Seljuk Turks expanding Tripoli's territory and securing its borders. He also maintained strong ties with the Crusader states particularly the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He participated in several Crusades including the Third Crusade where he served as a military commander. His military campaigns were often costly but ultimately successful in preserving Tripoli's independence and autonomy. He also engaged in diplomatic negotiations seeking to secure favorable terms for his realm.\n\n*Political and Economic Policies* Raymond III was a shrewd administrator known for his emphasis on stability and economic development. He fostered trade and commerce promoting the growth of Tripoli's economy. He invested in infrastructure building roads and fortifications to facilitate trade and communication. He also encouraged the growth of agriculture and industry. He implemented policies designed to improve the lives of his subjects including the construction of hospitals and schools. His governance was characterized by a blend of military strength and administrative efficiency creating a relatively peaceful and prosperous environment within Tripoli. He also worked to maintain a balance between local autonomy and adherence to the authority of the Crusader states.\n\n*Legacy and Death* Raymond III died in 1203 at the age of 58. His reign is considered a golden age for Tripoli a period of relative peace prosperity and cultural flourishing. He is remembered as a skilled military leader a wise administrator and a dedicated protector of his people. His contributions to the defense of the Levant and the economic development of Tripoli left a lasting impact on the region. He is often portrayed as a model ruler a symbol of strength and resilience in a turbulent era. His legacy continues to be studied by historians who view him as a pivotal figure in the history of the Crusader states and the Levant.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.14342188835144043}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -4.5060096454108134e-05}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁was", "logprob": -0.0798519179224968}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.04111293703317642}, {"token": "▁prominent", "logprob": -1.4056142568588257}, {"token": "▁figure", "logprob": -0.026309851557016373}, {"token": "▁in", "logprob": -0.005748288705945015}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.03307735174894333}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -0.8263755440711975}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.0019922663923352957}, 